WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.800
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:12.800
I cannot tell you how excited I am by this&nbsp;
paper. Wow. Today you will see more incredible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:21.920
images generated by an AI. However, not from&nbsp;
OpenAI, but Google! Just a few months ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:27.840
OpenAI’s image generator AI called&nbsp;
DALL-E 2 took the world by storm.

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:34.800
You could name almost anything, Cat Napoleon,&nbsp;
a teddy bear on a skateboard on Times Square,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:40.960
a basketball player dunking as an explosion&nbsp;
of a nebula, and it was able to create an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:46.880
appropriate image for it. However, there was&nbsp;
one interesting thing about it. What do you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:56.560
think the prompt for this must have been? Hmm.&nbsp;
Not easy, right? Well, it was “A sign that says&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.560 --> 00:01:02.800
deep learning”. Oh yes, this was one of&nbsp;
the failure cases. Please remember this.

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:08.720
Now, we always say that in research, do&nbsp;
not look at where we are, always look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:14.160
at where we will be two more papers down&nbsp;
the line. However, we didn’t even make it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:19.360
two more papers down the line. What’s more,&nbsp;
we barely made two months down the line,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:26.000
and here it is: this is Google Research’s&nbsp;
incredible image generator AI, Imagen.

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:31.760
This technique also looks at millions&nbsp;
of image and text description pairs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:38.560
and learns what people mean when they&nbsp;
say this is a guitar, or this is a panda.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:47.520
But, the magic happens here. Oh yes, it also&nbsp;
learned how to combine these concepts together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:54.000
and how a panda would play a guitar. The&nbsp;
frets and the strings seem a little wacky,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.000 --> 00:02:01.440
but what do we know, this is not human&nbsp;
engineering. This is panda engineering. Or a robot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:06.960
engineering something for a panda.&nbsp;
We are living crazy times indeed.

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:12.480
So, what is different here? Why do&nbsp;
we need another image generator AI?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:21.520
Well, let’s pop the hood, and look inside. Oh yes.&nbsp;
Two things that will immediately make a difference&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:29.440
come to mind. One, this architecture is simpler.&nbsp;
Two, it learns on longer text descriptions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:35.840
and hopefully that also means that it can&nbsp;
generate text better. Let’s have a look.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:43.200
What? Are you seeing what I am seeing?&nbsp;
That is not just some text, that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:49.360
is a beautiful piece of text, exactly what&nbsp;
we were looking for. Absolutely amazing.

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:55.440
And, these not the only advantages, you know, I&nbsp;
am a light transport researcher by trade, and I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.440 --> 00:03:05.040
promised to myself that I’ll try not to flip out.&nbsp;
But…hold on to your papers, and… holy mother of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:12.640
papers. Look at that. It can also generate&nbsp;
beautiful refractive objects. That duck is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:20.400
truly a sight to behold. My goodness. Now I will&nbsp;
note that DALL-E 2 was also pretty good at this.

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:28.160
And, if we plug in DeepMind’s new Flamingo&nbsp;
language model, would you look at that!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:34.640
Is this really happening? Yes, that is an&nbsp;
AI commenting on a different AI’s work.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.440 --> 00:03:40.560
What a time to be alive! We will have a&nbsp;
look at this paper too in the near future,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:46.320
make sure to subscribe and hit the bell icon,&nbsp;
you really don’t want to miss it when it comes.

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:53.360
And, you know what, let’s test it some more&nbsp;
against OpenAI’s amazing DALL-E 2. See how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.360 --> 00:04:00.560
they stack up against each other. The first prompt&nbsp;
will be a couple of glasses sitting on the table.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:10.000
Well, with Google’s Imagen, oh my, these ones are&nbsp;
amazing, once again, proper refractive objects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:16.800
loving it. And, what about DALL-E 2? There is one&nbsp;
with the glasses that with an interesting framing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.520 --> 00:04:25.920
but I see both reflections and refractions,&nbsp;
apart from the framing, I am liking this. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:34.400
the rest…well, yes, those are glasses sitting on&nbsp;
the table. But when we say a couple of glasses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:42.960
we probably mean these and not these. But that’s&nbsp;
really interesting, two AIs that have a linguistic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:49.200
battle here. Imagine showing this to someone&nbsp;
just ten years ago. See how they would react.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:57.280
Loving it. Also, I bet that in the future, these&nbsp;
AIs will work like brands and products today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.280 --> 00:05:01.680
where people will have strong&nbsp;
opinions as to which ones they prefer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:07.360
The analog warmth of Imagen, or the&nbsp;
three-year warranty on DALL-E 4?

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:13.200
And wait, you are all experienced Fellow&nbsp;
Scholars here, so you also wish to see the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:19.600
two tested against each other a little more&nbsp;
rigorously. And we’ll do exactly that. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:27.040
paper checks the new technique against previous&nbsp;
results mathematically. Or, we can ask a bunch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:35.120
of humans which one they prefer. And, wow. The&nbsp;
new technique passes with flying colors on both.

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:43.120
And once again, DALL-E 2 appeared just about 2&nbsp;
months ago, and now, a new followup paper from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.120 --> 00:05:49.440
Google that tests really well against it.&nbsp;
This is not two more papers down the line.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:56.240
Not even two more years down the line.&nbsp;
This is just two more months down the line.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.240 --> 00:06:03.280
And a year before, we had DALL-E 1, and&nbsp;
see how much of a difference OpenAI made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.280 --> 00:06:09.520
in just a year. Now I am almost certain that&nbsp;
this paper has been in the works for a while,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.520 --> 00:06:16.160
and they added the comparisons against DALL-E&nbsp;
2 at the end. But still, a followup paper this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:24.640
quickly? The pace of progress in AI research is&nbsp;
absolutely incredible. What a time to be alive!

00:06:25.200 --> 00:06:28.960
So, does this get your mind&nbsp;
going? What else would you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.960 --> 00:06:32.320
use this new technique for? Let&nbsp;
